    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup for the **Austin Waves** is anchored by a high-efficiency outlook from **Jackson Merrill** (75.27), who serves as our premier start against Sasaki. **Jackson Merrill** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against a starter with a 4.56 xERA and, crucially, a slow delivery that projects for a stolen base opportunity, directly addressing our organizational mandate to chase speed. **Kyle Tucker** (70.00) and **Nasim Nunez** (65.68) follow closely as elite plays; **Nasim Nunez** is particularly enticing despite batting eighth, as the combination of his switch-hitting profile and the opposing battery's vulnerability to the stolen base allows him to transcend his lower-order placement. Meanwhile, **Andy Pages** (64.69) and **Josh Jung** (63.87) provide strong core production, leveraging positive SP skill matchups to maintain their status as reliable daily contributors.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s board involved **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (51.11), who narrowly missed the starting cut in favor of higher-efficiency options. While **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** remains a staple of the roster, his matchup against a high-tier arm like Eovaldi (3.36 xERA) suppressed his base projection, making him the odd man out in a crowded utility and corner infield pool. Risks for the day remain concentrated on our platoon-sensitive bats: **Gunnar Henderson** (58.44) and **Coby Mayo** (57.48) both face the southpaw Alvarez, resulting in a drag on their offensive potential despite the park factors. We are monitoring **Josh Lowe** closely; while his platoon advantage against Ginn is favorable, his ninth-order placement and the risk posed by an elite defensive catcher suggest that any volatility in the early innings could necessitate an aggressive pinch-hit approach later in the game.",
